
  [1mThe fork:[0m how tasks are grouped, and therefore who can see which ones.

  - [1mOne shared workspace[0m — everyone in the team sees every task. Simplest possible authorization: are you a member, yes
    or no. Because visibility carries no signal (you can see everything), "tasks they care about" has to be [3mexplicit[0m —
    you're assigned, you created it, or you deliberately watched it. That's actually a clean notification story.
  - [1mProjects / lists with membership[0m — tasks live in projects, people join projects. Visibility now carries signal, so
    project membership becomes an implicit relevance input alongside explicit watching. More moving parts, and
    notification relevance gets two sources that can disagree.
  - [1mPer-task sharing[0m — you invite specific people to individual tasks. Most granular, most complex; an authorization
    check on every read. I'd need a strong reason to recommend this.

  Cost of changing later, stated honestly: going from one workspace to projects is a real migration — it touches the
  authorization layer, every query, and the notification relevance rules. It's not free. But it's [3madditive[0m, whereas
  starting with projects you don't need means paying for that complexity in every query from day one and possibly never
  using it.

  [1mMy recommendation: one shared workspace[0m, on YAGNI grounds. It makes "cares about" explicit rather than inferred, which
  is the clearer notification model anyway — a person is notified because of a relationship they can see and control,
  not because of a group membership they've forgotten they have. If you already know you have multiple distinct teams or
  clients who must not see each other's tasks, that's a hard requirement and overrides this — tell me and we go to
  projects.
